@page "/docs/primitives/accessibility"

<DocsPage Title="Accessibility" Description="Learn how to make the most of the primitives' accessibility features and guidelines.">
    <p>
        The primitives handle many of the complex details of accessibility: roles and ARIA attributes, pointer interactions,
        keyboard navigation and focus management. They aim for an accessible experience out of the box, with parameters to
        configure it. This page covers what they do, and what's still up to you.
    </p>
    <Callout>
        The primitives haven't been tested with screen readers or voice control, and they haven't been audited. Keyboard use,
        focus management, ARIA state and axe scans have been checked in Chromium and Firefox. If your project needs to be
        accessible, test it yourself with the assistive technology your users have.
    </Callout>

    <DocsHeading Title="Keyboard navigation" />
    <p>
        Each primitive follows its <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/">WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices</a> pattern and
        its keyboard behaviour. That matters most to people who can't use a pointer, and to everyone who prefers the
        keyboard. Many primitives use the arrow keys, letters for typeahead, <kbd>Home</kbd>, <kbd>End</kbd>,
        <kbd>Enter</kbd> and <kbd>Esc</kbd>.
    </p>

    <DocsHeading Title="Focus management" />
    <p>
        The primitives move focus for you after an interaction: into a popup when it opens, around a group with the arrow
        keys, and back to the trigger when the popup closes. Popups take <code>InitialFocus</code> and
        <code>FinalFocus</code> parameters to choose where focus goes instead.
    </p>
    <p>
        Focus also follows what Blazor renders. If Blazor removes the focused item, focus moves to the item now in its place
        rather than to the page, and closing or disabling a part from code returns focus to its trigger.
    </p>
    <p>
        It's up to you to show where focus is, usually by styling <code>:focus-visible</code>, and the highlighted item of
        a menu or list (<code>data-highlighted</code>). For WCAG AA, the indicator needs a contrast of at least 3:1 with what
        it sits on (<a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/non-text-contrast">non-text contrast</a>), as do
        the borders that show where a control is and the marks that show its state, such as a checked box or a pressed
        toggle. WCAG also has <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/focus-appearance">guidelines on focus
        appearance</a>. An outline stays visible in Windows' high contrast mode (forced colors), where box shadows and
        backgrounds can disappear.
    </p>

    <DocsHeading Title="Color contrast" />
    <p>
        When you style the parts, meet the minimum contrast between each foreground and its background. Unless your app has
        strict requirements to comply with current standards, consider <a href="https://apcacontrast.com/">APCA</a>.
    </p>

    <DocsHeading Title="Accessible labels" />
    <p>
        <a href="/docs/primitives/form">Form</a>, <a href="/docs/primitives/field">Field</a>,
        <a href="/docs/primitives/fieldset">Fieldset</a> and <a href="/docs/primitives/input">Input</a> associate labels,
        descriptions and errors with their controls for you, and a native <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> works as well.
    </p>
    <p>
        A plain <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> works too, wherever it can't reach the element that takes focus by itself:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="/docs/primitives/checkbox">Checkbox</a>, <a href="/docs/primitives/switch">Switch</a> and
            <a href="/docs/primitives/radio-group">Radio</a> render a <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>, which no label can name. A
            <code>&lt;label for&gt;</code> at the <code>id</code> you give the control, or a <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> around
            it, names it through <code>aria-labelledby</code>, set once the page is interactive.
        </li>
        <li>
            A <a href="/docs/primitives/slider">Slider</a>'s thumbs take a <code>&lt;label for&gt;</code> at the root's
            <code>id</code>, or an <code>aria-label</code> or <code>aria-labelledby</code> on the root. Name the thumbs of a
            range one by one with <code>aria-label</code> on each <code>SliderThumb</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
            The list of options of a <a href="/docs/primitives/select">Select</a> or
            <a href="/docs/primitives/combobox">Combobox</a> is named after the label of its trigger or input.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        An <code>aria-label</code> or <code>aria-labelledby</code> you pass always wins over the one a part would set.
    </p>
    <p>
        Most apps also have controls whose accessible name has to come from markup, such as <code>alt</code>,
        <code>aria-label</code> or <code>aria-labelledby</code>: a button with only an icon, or a select with no visible
        label. A trigger with <code>role="combobox"</code> isn't named by its text, which is its value. WAI-ARIA has
        guidelines on <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/wai-aria-1.2/#namecalculation">providing accessible names</a> to
        custom controls.
    </p>

    <DocsHeading Title="Dragging" />
    <p>
        Anything that changes by dragging needs a way to do the same with a single click or tap
        (<a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/dragging-movements">WCAG 2.5.7</a>). A slider's track and a
        scroll area's track jump where you press them, a carousel has previous and next buttons, and a drawer closes from
        its close button, its backdrop or <kbd>Esc</kbd>. Where you build something else that drags, add the alternative
        yourself.
    </p>

    <DocsHeading Title="Server rendering" />
    <p>
        Every role and ARIA state is rendered by Blazor, not added later by JavaScript, so it's in the HTML from the first
        render, prerendered or not. The JavaScript only does what has to happen inside a browser event, such as moving focus
        and positioning popups, and reports back to Blazor, which keeps the state.
    </p>
</DocsPage>
